                <pb facs="0407" n="235" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg235"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 862.20212/70</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Fletcher</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mexico City</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-02-26T22:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 26, 1917, 10 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 27, 5 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9. Your telegram 10, February 26, 11 a.m. General Carranza is in Jalisco. I
                    called upon Minister for Foreign Affairs this evening and read to him the
                    substance of the German note. He said that he had no knowledge of any
                    representations of the character referred to and that if such representations
                    were made they must have been made directly to Carranza; that the German
                    Minister had several times recently asked him when Carranza would return to the
                    city. Minister for Foreign Affairs went directly to the telegraph office to
                    confer with Carranza. At the Minister’s urgent request I gave him a synopsis of
                    German note in Spanish to be used in his telegraphic conference with Carranza. I
                    also suggest going to see General Carranza if deemed desirable. Will report
                    further developments as soon as possible.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Fletcher</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
